WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:07.840
SHANE GLASS: Hey, there, and
welcome to Google Cloud Next

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:08.920
On Air.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:12.070
Today we'll be talking about
how public data could help data

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:15.910
scientists, researchers,
public health agencies,

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:19.810
and others combat the novel
2019 coronavirus, also known

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.270
as COVID-19, and help
flatten the curve

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.600
of cases around the globe.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:26.670
My name is Shane Glass.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:28.650
I'm a developer advocate
here at Google Cloud,

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.020
covering business
intelligence, and I'm also

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.990
the tech lead for the cloud
public data sets program.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.940
I'm thankful to be joined today
by my co-presenter Javier.

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.273
Javier, you want to take a
minute to introduce yourself?

00:00:39.273 --> 00:00:40.315
JAVIER DE LA TORRE: Sure.

00:00:40.315 --> 00:00:41.020
Hi, everybody.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:43.260
My name is Javier de
la Torre, and then

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:46.290
the co-founder of CARTO.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.522
SHANE GLASS: Awesome,
thanks, Javier.

00:00:48.522 --> 00:00:50.980
So here's a quick outline of
what we intend to cover today.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.660
So let's start with a quick
overview of Google Cloud's

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.120
public data sets program.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.840
We currently offer more than
180 public machine learning

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:07.180
ready data sets available
in our products,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:10.810
both in structured data sets in
our serverless data warehouse

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:13.150
BigQuery and
unstructured data sets,

00:01:13.150 --> 00:01:16.560
like satellite imagery
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.210
And we work hard to ensure
that each of these data sets

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.180
are as up to date as possible
according to the requirements

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:24.040
of each individual data set.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.960
Sometimes this means
real time updates,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.170
like our cryptocurrency
blockchains,

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:31.840
and sometimes this means annual
updates, like US Census Bureau

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.690
boundaries.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:36.480
We're also working with
our partners, team members,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.170
and customers to identify new
data sets of interest that

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.540
can expand our catalog as well.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.030
So ultimately, what
does this mean for you?

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.470
There's no need
to worry about ETL

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:49.830
for some of the most high value
public data sets in the world.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.140
We preload these core
data sets, and it

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.680
makes life easier for the data
analyst and data scientist

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.140
by reducing time to
get started and helping

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.330
you unlock new insights
in your existing data.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.890
Now as the COVID-19
pandemic truly

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.690
began to spread
across the globe,

00:02:06.690 --> 00:02:09.330
we recognized the need
for expanded access

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.580
to analyze data that
documents the spread

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.160
of the and its impacts.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.040
In March of 2020, we
announced our COVID-19

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.620
public data sets program,
which now contains

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.930
more than 20 data sets.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.930
To make it as easy as possible
for researchers and data

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:28.080
scientists to combat
COVID-19, these data sets

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.570
are preloaded into
BigQuery and continually

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.870
updated to reduce the
time to analyze the data.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.450
In addition to the
free access we provide

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.610
for all public
data sets, queries

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:42.090
against COVID-19 public data
sets are provided at no cost

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:45.990
through September 15 to maximize
the ability for researchers

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.220
to fight COVID-19.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.550
These include data sets
detailing COVID-19 confirmed

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:53.525
cases and deaths
around the world,

00:02:53.525 --> 00:02:55.150
like those available
from "The New York

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.150
Times," the Italian Department
of Civil Protection,

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:00.280
and the European Center
for Disease and Prevention

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.790
and Control, data sets that
contain information about how

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:08.420
governments are acting to combat
COVID-19, such as the Oxford

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.170
COVID-19 Government Response
Tracker, that describes policy

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:14.240
actions that have been
put into place to mitigate

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.150
the impacts of
COVID-19, and data

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:18.990
sets that can
contextualize outbreaks,

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.360
such as the US Census Bureau's
American Community Survey.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.250
We think this can
truly make a difference

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:26.360
in flattening the curve.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.340
Let's pause here for a
second for those unfamiliar

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.750
with BigQuery.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:36.080
BigQuery is Google Cloud's
highly scalable enterprise data

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.890
warehouse solution.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:39.770
It's fully managed
and serverless,

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.650
offers real time insights
from streaming data,

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.290
has built ML out
of the box, and has

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.100
a high speed in memory BI
Engine for faster and reporting

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.090
and analysis.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.730
BigQuery is designed to
make data analysts and data

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.940
scientists more
productive, and it's

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:59.010
a secure offering that offers
data encryption by default

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.350
both in transit and
at rest to ensure

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:05.280
that your data are protected.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.760
So let's get back to the
public data to combat COVID-19.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.880
Now we believe that the
COVID-19 public data sets

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.720
we are hosting, combined with
the high powered analysis

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.420
platforms like
BigQuery and CARTO,

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:21.649
can help support the mitigation
of the impact of COVID-19

00:04:21.649 --> 00:04:24.830
in three primary ways.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:27.900
The first of these is informing
individual decision making.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:31.040
And in my opinion, this
might be the most important.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.320
This is critical to slowing
the spread of the virus

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.970
by helping
individuals understand

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.070
the importance of
social distancing,

00:04:37.070 --> 00:04:40.310
the importance of wearing a
mask, and other safeguards.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.280
It can also help researchers and
public health officials better

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.385
understand which policy actions
are having a positive impact.

00:04:49.303 --> 00:04:50.720
Now, the second
of these use cases

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.910
is analyzing the second
order of public health

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.010
impacts of the pandemic.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.380
Secondary public health
impacts of the virus

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.010
include potential food security
among employees and industries

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:04.340
that are most likely to be
displaced by the COVID-19

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:06.740
impact on the economy,
according to the public health

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:08.840
officials we've worked with.

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:10.310
Now these data
sets are typically

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.950
used by public health officials
to identify people and regions

00:05:13.950 --> 00:05:16.430
that are dealing with
impacts of the pandemic other

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:18.200
than the virus itself.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:22.070
Data sets such as Google's
Community Mobility Reports,

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:24.920
the Census Bureau's
American Community Survey,

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:27.680
and global health and population
trends from the World Bank

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:31.070
are crucial to help public
health officials better

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.820
understand and ensure that
the full public health impact

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:38.300
of the pandemic is mitigated.

00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.040
And finally, we're
helping inform

00:05:40.040 --> 00:05:43.070
individual economic decision
making, such as helping

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:45.680
governments make an informed
data driven decision

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.810
about reopening economies.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.970
Combining these data
sets, such as data

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:53.510
about COVID-19 cases
from USA Facts,

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:57.140
the Oxford university Government
Policy Response Tracker,

00:05:57.140 --> 00:05:59.660
aggregated hospital capacity
from the American Hospital

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:03.770
Association, and Geotab's
commercial mobility impacts,

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:05.690
government officials
and businesses

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:09.140
can make an informed decision
about the risks and benefits

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.450
of reopening their economies.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.510
This can help states, such as
the five Western states that

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:18.200
make up the Western states pack,
take a data driven approach

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:21.140
to measuring their published
criteria for reopening

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:24.530
economies, including
the ability of hospitals

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.890
to handle a new wave of
patients and protect those most

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:31.560
vulnerable to COVID-19.

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:34.370
So let's take a look at some
examples of how Google Cloud

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.310
customers are already
seeing success

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:40.400
using COVID-19 public data
sets in the three categories

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.160
we've laid out.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.620
The first of these is
the state of Oklahoma.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:47.870
State officials in Oklahoma
collaborated with teams

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:50.750
from across Google Cloud
to develop and launch

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.600
a Looker dashboard that helps
keep their citizens informed

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:55.590
of the current status.

00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:58.010
This helps reinforce the
importance of public health

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:00.890
efforts by providing
citizens transparency

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:03.500
into the decision making
process and the impacts

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:06.740
of these decisions.

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:10.030
The second of these is analyzing
the second order public health

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:12.310
impacts of the pandemic.

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:15.060
Descartes Labs has built
and widely published

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.940
an analysis that shows
the reduction of pollution

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:19.740
and other emissions
within areas that

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:22.480
have high number
of COVID-19 cases

00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:24.360
and that are currently
under stay at home

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:25.425
or safer at home orders.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.770
And the final example is
from the Emergent Alliance,

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:32.430
a data analytics
coalition that's

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.230
spearheaded by Rolls Royce.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.870
The Emergent Alliance
is a response

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.150
from a growing number
of global brands

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.970
who believe in using the power
of big data for the greater

00:07:41.970 --> 00:07:43.320
good.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:46.230
By combining private
and public data sets,

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:49.500
the Emergent Alliance aims
to accelerate and smooth

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:52.890
economic recovery from
the COVID-19 crisis.

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:56.250
Their stated mission is to
help governments, businesses,

00:07:56.250 --> 00:07:59.490
and communities help each
other in the recovery

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.790
and provide better insights
through better data that

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:05.460
provide better
outcomes for everybody.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.730
Now a lot of this amazing
work is only possible

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:10.950
because of the powerful
analytics platforms

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.240
that are available.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:15.030
Now one of these is CARTO,
a leading spatial analytics

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:17.350
company headquartered
in New York City.

00:08:17.350 --> 00:08:19.770
I'm joined today all the
way from Madrid, Spain

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.850
by their founder and chief
strategy officer, Javier de la

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:25.920
Torre, to talk a
little more about how

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.870
they are helping their
customers leverage

00:08:27.870 --> 00:08:30.510
public data to combat COVID-19.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:31.973
Javier.

00:08:31.973 --> 00:08:33.390
JAVIER DE LA TORRE:
Thanks, Shane.

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:36.280
I'm pretty excited to be here
today collaborating with Google

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:38.340
on COVID-19 mitigation.

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:41.610
We have seen an explosion on
usage of maps and location

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.820
intelligence to fight COVID-19.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:47.310
It's like the entire population
had cut almost a 101 course

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:49.610
of maps and mobility recently.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:51.800
And that's because,
I'd like to say,

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:54.510
there's never been a more
important time to geography.

00:08:54.510 --> 00:08:57.030
Understanding the
geography of the pandemic

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.980
and the mobility
of the population

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.080
is one of the few tools
that we have right now

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:03.160
available to fight COVID.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:06.570
At Carto, we have been pretty
busy over the last months,

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:08.070
at the beginning
of the pandemic,

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:10.440
working with different
governments and organizations

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:12.000
on the response to COVID.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.530
And BigQuery has been
a fundamental part

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:18.320
to handle very large spatial
datasets on these use cases.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.000
But before [INAUDIBLE]
we do that,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.600
I just want to give you a
little quick intro about Carto.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.910
So Carto is a location
intelligence platform.

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:31.700
We help organizations analyze
and visualize location data.

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.900
Let me tell you a little
bit about what it means.

00:09:34.900 --> 00:09:38.410
One way that I like to explain
what location is-- location

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:40.600
intelligence is,
is by explaining

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.970
the difference between
understanding where

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.800
and understanding why.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:46.750
You want to look
at where there's

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:50.590
more COVID-19 cases, you
will likely use a BI tool,

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.040
such as Looker.

00:09:52.040 --> 00:09:53.950
But if you want
to understand why

00:09:53.950 --> 00:09:57.460
there are more cases on one
place versus another, if you

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:01.180
want to understand what are the
characteristics of those places

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:04.540
that makes them feel
more vulnerable to COVID,

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:07.360
then you need to create what
we call a spatial model.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.880
And for doing that,
you need Carto.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.250
So with Carto,
think about creating

00:10:12.250 --> 00:10:14.290
spatial models that
help you understand

00:10:14.290 --> 00:10:18.240
why something happened so.

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:20.050
Now we provide
these capabilities

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:22.110
in a product with
different interfaces.

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:25.960
We work with data scientists,
analysts, and developers.

00:10:25.960 --> 00:10:29.420
And more important, I think very
relevant in this case, Carto.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:32.800
We like to say, comes also
with batteries included.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:37.000
Most of our users use Carto
with data observatory, which

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:41.050
is our repository of spatial
data, things like demographics,

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.110
mobility, financial data,
that is useful for doing

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.445
a lot of this analysis.

00:10:45.445 --> 00:10:47.320
A lot of these datasets
will be collaborating

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:49.680
with the BigQuery
public datasets,

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.400
to make them available
to the Query community.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:57.470
Now more widely, Carto
and Google Cloud Platform

00:10:57.470 --> 00:11:01.680
provides a full solution of
what we call a next generation

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.780
spatial data infrastructure.

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:07.250
With components like the
AI Platform and BigQuery,

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.860
Google Cloud Platform
together with Carto,

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.170
provides a great
new way of thinking

00:11:12.170 --> 00:11:13.630
about the spatial analysis.

00:11:13.630 --> 00:11:15.470
And we have been
leveraging it, like we

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:17.267
said, a lot during COVID-19.

00:11:17.267 --> 00:11:18.725
So let me show you
a few use cases.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:23.890
So the first one, as
we were describing,

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:28.290
combining location data
with COVID-19 cases,

00:11:28.290 --> 00:11:31.180
government can actually create
what we call risk models.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:34.030
It can do risk
analysis to understand

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:36.880
which areas are more
vulnerable depending

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:39.580
on things like
demographics, like age,

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:43.090
to have a bigger
effect COVID-19.

00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:44.980
Right, so these
type of analysis are

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:49.450
possible by combining a lot
of the datasets, some Shane,

00:11:49.450 --> 00:11:52.210
you were talking before.

00:11:52.210 --> 00:11:54.860
Now, another use case
that I like a lot,

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:57.240
and you put it before very well.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.700
There's been an incredible
pressure on governments

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:05.280
to explain the reasons why
they are closing up or open up,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:08.060
those lockdowns having
affected so many people.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:10.070
And in order to
do that, they had

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:13.870
to be very responsible in the
way they publicize the data,

00:12:13.870 --> 00:12:15.940
and how they explain
the stories behind it.

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.410
So helping to tell those
stories and make trends

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:23.400
get transparency, so that
makes it way more effective,

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:26.550
and makes it citizens
understands the importance

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:30.030
of those measurements.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:33.240
Of course, another
typical call or use case

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.450
that we've been seeing, is
associating human mobility,

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.260
with changes on the economy.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.970
We know when lockdowns
happens, the economy shrinks.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.750
So trying to understand the
correlation between these two

00:12:45.750 --> 00:12:49.840
factors, is a pretty
important factor.

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.770
It can help you to collect,
understand, and mitigate

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:56.590
